    "why_ka": {
      "type": "why-ka",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "Every Indian language begins with the same letter",
        "body": "<p>India's languages aren't dialects of one another — many come from entirely different families. Naming a chai company after any one of them would have meant leaving everyone else out.</p><p>But all of them teach the alphabet the same way. The vowels come first, then the consonants — <em>ka, kha, ga, gha</em> — and every script starts that list with the same sound.</p><p>So we named the company after the one letter they share. It is the ring around the cup in our logo, and the letters running down the side of this page.</p>",
        "show_glyphs": false,
        "glyph_alt": "The letter ka, written in thirteen Indian scripts",
        "caption": "“Ka”, in thirteen Indian scripts. The same sound, and almost nothing in common.",
        "closing": "An Indian company, built for America. All of it.",
        "color_scheme": "scheme-8",
        "section_width": "page-width",
        "padding-block-start": 80,
        "padding-block-end": 80,
        "show_pattern": true,
        "pattern_opacity": 12
      }
    }
